<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paracelsus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1493">1493</a>–<a href="/years/1541">1541</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Theophrastus Bombast von Hohenheim</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paracelsus, born Theophrastus Bombast von Hohenheim at Einsiedeln in 1493, was a Swiss physician, alchemist, lay theologian and philosopher of the German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. He studied medicine at the University of Basel and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, took his doctorate at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, and between 1517 and 1524 travelled widely across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, serving as an army surgeon. A pioneer of the medical revolution of the Renaissance, he stressed observation alongside received wisdom and is credited as the father of toxicology. His Prognostications were studied by Rosicrucians in the 17th century, and the medical movement inspired by his works is known as Paracelsianism. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> in 1541.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paracelsus.jpg" alt="Paracelsus" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Quinten Metsys · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paracelsus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
